acceptance.how_to_verify:
node -e '
const {readFileSync}=require("node:fs");
const css=readFileSync("src/web/styles.css","utf8").replace(/\/\*[\s\S]*?\*\//g," ");
const hits=[];
for(const m of css.matchAll(/([^{}]+)\{([^{}]*)\}/g))
  for(const d of m[2].matchAll(/(^|;)\s*overflow-wrap\s*:\s*([^;]+)/g))
    if(m[1].split(",").map(s=>s.trim()).some(p=>p===".awaiting-list"||p===".awaiting-list li"))
      hits.push([m[1].trim(), d[2].trim(), /(^|;)\s*display\s*:\s*grid\s*(;|$)/.test(m[2])]);
console.log(JSON.stringify(hits));
' — expected exactly [[".awaiting-list li","anywhere",true]], and the same command on the unmodified file must return []. I ran both on this tree: [] on the shipped file, and the expected triple on a scratch copy carrying the declaration in place. Do NOT check `grep -c overflow-wrap`, `grep -n overflow-wrap | wc -l`, or `grep -c 'overflow-wrap:'` in either direction: all three are counts whose value depends on whether the mandated comment names the property in prose, and the pre-existing comment above the four-card rule happens to have no colon after the property name, which is an accident and not a reason.

node -e '
const src=require("node:fs").readFileSync("test/web/pages.test.js","utf8").split("\n");
const PHRASE="the h1 carries the page-title hook";
const hits=src.map((l,i)=>[i+1,l]).filter(([,l])=>l.includes(PHRASE)||l.includes(`class="page-title"`));
const first=src.findIndex(l=>l.includes(PHRASE))+1;
console.log("hits:", hits.map(([n])=>n).join(","));
console.log("first phrase line:", first, JSON.stringify(src[first-1]));
console.log("ok:", hits.length===6 && /assert\.match\(html, H1_HOOK,/.test(src[first-1]));
' — expect `ok: true`. The two halves are what matter and each is shape, not number: the first line in the file containing the phrase is the `assert.match(html, H1_HOOK,` line, and the set of lines matching either string is exactly the six pre-existing ones. Run it immediately after writing the block, which files[](1)(5) requires for this reason. On the UNMODIFIED tree the same command already prints `ok: true`; it is the AFTER state that is the check, and it must be indistinguishable from the before state except for the line the helpers moved.
